Z||000000||Raydar||08-30-2001||01:24 PM||||Anyone know if the GM fuel sending unit is still available? What about aftermarket?<BR>Part numbers? <P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||08-30-2001||11:17 PM||||Anybody?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||08-31-2001||02:16 AM||||For $385.00 you can order the sending unit from a dealer. I don't know if there is an aftermarket one available. I grabbed one out of a Fiero in a junk yard and cleaned it up and replaced the wire. Don't mess with the coil near the bottom, I just cleaned the outside off with TV Tuner spray. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||08-31-2001||07:16 AM||||If the reason for replacement is an incorrect fuel indication, you can most likely repair it. The early cars used a wirewound variable resistor and the later ones used a carbon resistor. You can clean the resistor with contact cleaner. If it's really bad you can use a pencil eraser to clean it. measure the resistance with an ohm-meter before you put it back in, it should have a nice steady sweep, I think from ~ 0-90 ohms. I have fixed a few of them that way.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||08-31-2001||07:34 AM||||Thanks, guys.<BR>A friend who did a "shotgun" fix for a bad fuel pump (replaced the whole tank with one from a parts car) has the most pressing need. Gauge has a mind of its own.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>I have the normal "empty at 1/4 tank" problem, with both of mine. <BR>Thanks again!<P>Raydar||||reg||14
Z||000005||RoadRocket||08-31-2001||05:19 PM||||I was told that you could not replace the fuel sending unit without replacing the entire tank. Is this so? If not, then where is the sending unit? How do I get to it to replace it?<P><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||08-31-2001||06:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RoadRocket:<BR><B>I was told that you could not replace the fuel sending unit without replacing the entire tank. Is this so? If not, then where is the sending unit? How do I get to it to replace it?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The sending unit and the float are one assembly. You need to drop the entire tank and pull it out the top. You don't need to replace the tank.||||reg||1
